From: Fabiano Tassotti <fabianotassotti@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] net: dsa: realtek: sync the conduit's own hardware VLAN filter on bridge-vlan membership changes

DSA's own "direct VLAN on a user port" path
(net/dsa/user.c:dsa_user_vlan_rx_add_vid()) calls both
dsa_port_vlan_add() (the switch's own VLAN table) and
dsa_port_host_vlan_add() for every VID it adds. The latter, besides
notifying the driver, also calls vlan_vid_add() on the conduit netdevice
itself - registering that VID in the conduit's own hardware RX VLAN
filter (net/dsa/port.c), which stmmac (this board's dwmac-ipq5018
conduit) implements and enables unconditionally whenever the MAC
reports VLAN hash filtering capability
(drivers/net/ethernet/stmicro/stmmac/stmmac_main.c,
NETIF_F_HW_VLAN_CTAG_FILTER, gated on priv->dma_cap.vlhash).

Generic Linux bridge VLAN filtering (switchdev, i.e. a VLAN-aware
bridge with vlan_filtering=1 and VLANs added through bridge-vlan
membership rather than a plain kernel VLAN device stacked directly on
a DSA user port) does not go through dsa_user_vlan_rx_add_vid() at
all, and therefore never calls dsa_port_host_vlan_add() or
vlan_vid_add() on the conduit. The switch itself gets correctly
configured (this driver's rtl8365mb_port_vlan_add()/_del() apply to
both paths identically, via the same dsa_switch_ops.port_vlan_add/del
hooks) and correctly forwards a VLAN-tagged frame to the CPU - but the
conduit's own hardware VLAN filter never learned that VID, so it
silently drops the frame at the MAC/DMA level before it ever reaches
the DSA tag-decoding layer. This shows up as counted, non-error
rx_dropped on the resulting VLAN sub-interface (e.g. a VLAN-aware
bridge's own "br<n>.<vid>" self-interface), not as any kind of
corruption or DSA-visible decode failure - the frame was fine, the
conduit chose not to pass it up.

Measured on a Mercusys MR80X v5 (RTL8367S-VB, family D, native RTL8_4
CPU tag - no 8021q tag protocol involved in this particular test):
a VLAN-aware test bridge with the WAN port tagged into a VLAN reached
"forwarding" state cleanly, the switch and conduit both showed healthy
RX activity, but the VLAN sub-interface's own DHCP client never
completed - rx_dropped counting up on exactly that interface, zero
rx_errors/rx_crc_errors/rx_frame_errors, i.e. a deliberate filter
drop, not corruption.

Fix it in the driver rather than relying on Linux's generic
"CPU/host VLAN add" plumbing to be reachable from every VLAN-membership
path (it currently isn't, for hardware-filtering conduits reached via
switchdev bridge VLANs): keep the conduit's own VLAN filter in sync
directly from rtl8365mb_port_vlan_add()/_del(), which both paths
(direct user-port VLAN and switchdev bridge VLAN) already call
identically. Every VID actually programmed into the switch's own
tables is also registered on the conduit, regardless of which UCI/
kernel mechanism asked for it.

v2: dsa_switch_host_vlan_add() (net/dsa/switch.c) replays this same
driver op for every dsa_port in the tree, including the CPU port's own
entry - which has no dp->cpu_dp of its own, since a CPU port isn't its
own CPU port. The first version of this patch dereferenced that
unconditionally and crashed with a NULL pointer fault in
dsa_port_to_conduit() the moment any bridge (even the plain default
br-lan, which always replays VLAN 1 on join regardless of
vlan_filtering) brought a port up - reproduced on real hardware.
Guard on dp && dp->cpu_dp before touching the conduit.

Signed-off-by: Fabiano Tassotti <fabianotassotti@gmail.com>
---
--- a/drivers/net/dsa/realtek/rtl8365mb_main.c
+++ b/drivers/net/dsa/realtek/rtl8365mb_main.c
@@ -2309,6 +2309,7 @@ static int rtl8365mb_port_vlan_add(stru
 {
 	bool untagged = !!(vlan->flags & BRIDGE_VLAN_INFO_UNTAGGED);
 	bool pvid = !!(vlan->flags & BRIDGE_VLAN_INFO_PVID);
+	struct dsa_port *dp;
 	u16 pvid_vid;
 	struct realtek_priv *priv = ds->priv;
 	int ret;
@@ -2344,6 +2345,23 @@ static int rtl8365mb_port_vlan_add(stru
 	ret = rtl8365mb_vlan_4k_port_add(ds, port, vlan, extack);
 	if (ret)
 		goto undo_set_pvid;
+
+	/* Generic switchdev bridge-vlan membership never reaches
+	 * dsa_port_host_vlan_add(), so the conduit's own hardware VLAN
+	 * filter (if it has one) would otherwise never learn this VID and
+	 * would silently drop tagged frames meant for the CPU. Keep it in
+	 * sync here instead, for every path that can add a VLAN.
+	 *
+	 * DSA replays this same op for the CPU port's own dsa_port entry
+	 * too (dsa_switch_host_vlan_add() walks every port, not just user
+	 * ports); that entry has no dp->cpu_dp of its own, so guard against
+	 * it instead of dereferencing a NULL cpu_dp inside
+	 * dsa_port_to_conduit().
+	 */
+	dp = dsa_to_port(ds, port);
+	if (dp && dp->cpu_dp)
+		vlan_vid_add(dsa_port_to_conduit(dp), htons(ETH_P_8021Q),
+			    vlan->vid);
 
 	ret = 0;
 	goto out_unlock;
@@ -2372,6 +2387,7 @@ static int rtl8365mb_port_vlan_del(stru
 {
 	bool untagged = !!(vlan->flags & BRIDGE_VLAN_INFO_UNTAGGED);
 	bool pvid = !!(vlan->flags & BRIDGE_VLAN_INFO_PVID);
+	struct dsa_port *dp;
 	struct realtek_priv *priv = ds->priv;
 	int ret;
 
@@ -2387,6 +2403,11 @@ static int rtl8365mb_port_vlan_del(stru
 	if (ret)
 		goto out_unlock;
 
+	dp = dsa_to_port(ds, port);
+	if (dp && dp->cpu_dp)
+		vlan_vid_del(dsa_port_to_conduit(dp), htons(ETH_P_8021Q),
+			    vlan->vid);
+
 	ret = rtl8365mb_vlan_4k_port_del(ds, port, vlan);
 	/* There is little incentive to try to undo the removal of PVID (if it
 	 * was really in use) as an error here might indicate the ASIC stopped
